.TH PEEKIN 1 2026-09-25 "PeekIn 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
peekin \- manage PeekIn face authentication (Look. Recognize. Unlock.)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B peekin
.RB [ \-U
.IR user ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-\-plain ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B peekin
enrolls, lists and removes face models, changes settings, tests the camera
and explains why face authentication would not start.
Face authentication itself happens in
.BR pam_peekin (8)
for sudo, the GNOME login and lock screens, and optionally polkit dialogs and
the text console.
The password always remains available.
.PP
Commands that read or change face models or settings need root, e.g. through
.BR sudo (8).
The graphical
.BR peekin-settings (1)
does the same through polkit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-U " user" "\fR, \fP\-\-user " user
The account to act on.
Default: the user who ran sudo, doas or pkexec, otherwise the current user.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-assume\-yes
Answer every question with yes.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Machine-friendly output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the commands and options.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR add " [\fIlabel\fP]"
Capture a face model: several samples of the face, taken while the user looks
at the camera.
Add one model per look, for example with and without glasses.
.TP
.B list
List the user's face models with their ID, label and date.
.TP
.BI remove " id"
Remove one face model.
.TP
.B clear
Remove all face models of the user.
.TP
.BR cameras " [\fB\-\-best\fP]"
List the cameras usable for face authentication, administrator-approved
infrared cameras first.
With
.BR \-\-best ,
print only the path of the camera automatic selection would use.
.TP
.BR "cameras \-\-attest" " \fIcamera\fP [\fB\-\-save \-\-evidence\fP \fItext\fP] [\fB\-\-any\-unit\fP]"
Show the approval entry of an infrared camera, read from the device itself.
With
.BR \-\-save ,
add it to
.I /etc/peekin/ir-profiles.json
(needs root);
.I text
says how you checked that it is the infrared sensor and that its emitter
lights up, or is
.B \-
to read it from standard input.
.B \-\-any\-unit
approves every unit of that model instead of this serial number.
Colour and virtual cameras are refused.
.TP
.BI "cameras \-\-revoke " camera
Withdraw the approval of a camera (needs root).
.TP
.BR test " [\fB\-\-seconds\fP \fIn\fP] [\fB\-\-until\-match\fP]"
Show live detection and match results, with the same threshold and
head-movement check as a real login.
Runs for
.I n
seconds (1 to 30, default 18), or until the user is accepted with
.BR \-\-until\-match .
In a dark room with a colour camera it raises the screen brightness the same
way a login does (see
.B brighten_screen
in
.BR peekin-config (5)).
.TP
.B doctor
Check the configuration, camera, face models, file permissions, PAM
integration, SELinux and the failed-attempt counter, and explain each problem
with the command that fixes it.
The exit status is 1 when something prevents face authentication.
.TP
.BI set " name value"
Change one setting in
.IR /etc/peekin/config.ini ,
for example
.BR "peekin set timeout 3" .
The file is validated before it is replaced; comments are kept.
.TP
.B config
Edit
.IR /etc/peekin/config.ini .
The editor
.RB ( $EDITOR ,
else nano, else vi) opens a private copy and runs as the account that called
.BR sudo ,
never as root, since any editor can run commands; without
.B SUDO_USER
(or when it names root) the command refuses to start.
The copy replaces the file only when every value in it is valid and nobody
changed the file in the meantime; it is written atomically and keeps the
mode of the file. An invalid copy can be edited again.
.B sudo \-E peekin config
keeps your
.BR EDITOR .
.TP
.BR disable " \fB1\fP|\fB0\fP"
Disable (1) or enable (0) face authentication system-wide.
.TP
.B unseal-models
Decrypt the encrypted face models into
.I /run/peekin/models
(done at boot by
.BR peekin-models.service ),
and encrypt any plaintext face model found in
.I /etc/peekin/models
first, removing it from the disk.
When
.B [core] tpm2_pcrs
changed since the face models were encrypted, they are encrypted again with
the new TPM binding (see
.BR peekin-config (5)).
An account whose face models cannot be decrypted is reported and keeps no
decrypted copy, so its face login stays off; the password keeps working.
The face models are sealed with
.BR systemd-creds (1)
using the TPM2 chip and the host key, or the host key alone without a TPM;
they are not bound to PCRs.
Every change made with
.BR add ,
.BR remove ,
.B clear
or the settings app is sealed again; a change that cannot be sealed is not
made.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/peekin/config.ini
Settings; see
.BR peekin-config (5).
.TP
.I /etc/peekin/models/
Face models, one file per user, encrypted with the TPM and the host key
.RI ( user .dat.cred;
see
.BR "unseal-models" ).
.TP
.I /run/peekin/models/
The decrypted face models, in memory; readable by root and the
.B peekin
service account only.
.TP
.I /etc/peekin/ir-profiles.json
Administrator attestations of infrared cameras (see
.B ir_only
in
.BR peekin-config (5)).
.TP
.I /usr/share/peekin/models/
The face detection (YuNet) and recognition (SFace) models.
.TP
.I /var/lib/peekin/failures/
Failed-attempt counters, one file per user.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo peekin add "Without glasses"
sudo peekin test \-\-until\-match
sudo peekin cameras \-\-attest /dev/video2 \-\-save \-\-evidence "IR node, emitter lit"
sudo peekin doctor
.fi
.SH SECURITY
Face recognition is a convenience, not a replacement for the password.
The head-movement check stops photos and still pictures on a phone, not a
video of the user cut to follow the turns asked for or replayed until its own
turns come up; it is not certified presentation-attack detection.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peekin-settings (1),
.BR peekin-config (5),
.BR pam_peekin (8)
